    --- precision; more than a few kilometers from the origin, do your math on `CFrame` instead.
    Position: vector
    --- The rotation of this part in world space, as angles in degrees about the Y, X and Z axes,
    --- applied in that order.
    Orientation: vector
    --- The size of this part in meters, along its own X, Y and Z axes.
    Size: vector
    --- The material this part is drawn with: a material loaded with `Material.load`, a copy made with
    --- `material:Clone()`, or nil. With nil, the part uses the engine's default material, which is
    --- white, non-metallic and fairly rough. A part has no color of its own. Its look comes from its
    --- material, and a part can change only the parameters that the material declares, with
    --- `SetMaterialParameter`. The default material declares `Color` and `Transparency`. Reading this
    --- property returns the material itself, not a copy; call `material:Clone()` to get one you can
    --- change. A `MeshPart` with no material is drawn as its model file describes, tinted by a `Color`
    --- parameter if you set one.
    Material: Material?
    --- The material parameters this part overrides, as a table keyed by name. Each read returns a new
    --- table that holds only the overridden values. The possible names are `Color`, `Transparency`,
    --- `Emissive`, `Metalness`, `Roughness`, `NormalScale` and `AlphaCutoff`. An override that the
    --- current material does not declare is kept but has no effect, so a part looks the same after its
    --- material is changed and changed back. Prefer `SetMaterialParameter`, which checks the name
    --- against the material. Assigning this property replaces the whole set and checks only that each
    --- entry is a known parameter with a value of the right type.
    MaterialParameters: MaterialParameters
    --- Whether this part casts a shadow, from the sun and from every light. Turn it off for effects
    --- that should not darken the scene, such as a muzzle flash, a trail or a marker on the ground. The
    --- part is still drawn and lit, and still receives shadows. A transparent part casts no shadow
    --- regardless of this value.
    CastShadow: boolean
    --- Whether this part's phase decides what it collides with. On, the default, it never collides with
    --- the characters and owned parts of players outside its phase, and does with those inside it. Off,
    --- it collides as any part does, and the game decides with `CollisionGroup` who stands on what.
    PhaseCollision: boolean
    --- Whether the part may sleep where no player is near. On a dedicated server, a `CharacterBody`
    --- with no player's focus within `dormant_radius` (`[server]` in `project.toml`; twice
    --- `StreamingService.LoadRadius` unless the game says) does not walk, and what it was asked to do
    --- (`Move`) waits until a player comes near. A player sees only within the load radius, so a
    --- character wakes before anybody can see it. `Never` keeps the part awake wherever it is. A host
    --- and a single-player game never sleep anything, nor does a world one of whose players has no
    --- character or focus: that player is sent the whole world.
    Dormancy: EnumDormancy
    --- Whether the part is asleep this tick because no player is near it (`Dormancy`). Decided where
    --- the physics step begins, from where the players were then.
    read Dormant: boolean
    --- Whether `Workspace:RaycastAsSeenBy` checks a shot against this part where the shooter's machine
    --- drew it, rather than where it is now. Every player's `Character` is checked that way without it.
    --- Set it on a moving target that is not a player's, such as an NPC, a vehicle or a dummy. Only the
    --- authority reads it, and it is not replicated.
    LagCompensated: boolean
    --- How much this part's changes come first when a player's budget cannot carry every change at once
    --- (`Player.ReplicationBudget`). 1 by default; 3 makes the part's turn come three times as fast,
    --- 0.25 four times as slowly, and 0 only once a second. A part a player's machine waits for is
    --- drawn where it was last sent, moving smoothly between the states it was sent, a little behind.
    --- Only the authority reads it, and it is not replicated. A part that does not change costs
    --- nothing, whatever its priority.
    ReplicationPriority: number
    --- How much this machine fades the part out of its own picture, from 0 to 1. At 0 the part is drawn
    --- as `Transparency` says; at 1 it is not drawn. It is never replicated and never saved, so use it
    --- to change what one player sees of a part that every player has, such as a tree between the
    --- camera and the character. The part keeps casting the shadow that its `Transparency` gives it.
    Fade: number
    --- Whether a `Decal` is drawn on this part. A decal projects onto everything inside its box; turn
    --- this off for parts that should stay clean, such as a character standing on a scorch mark. The
    --- decal still paints the surfaces around the part. Terrain always receives decals.
    ReceivesDecals: boolean
    --- Whether this part is fixed in place. An anchored part is not moved by gravity, forces or
    --- collisions, but other parts still collide with it. Setting it keeps the part's position and
    --- orientation.
    Anchored: boolean
    --- Whether this part blocks other parts. With it off, other parts pass through, but `Touched` still
    --- fires, which is how a trigger volume, a checkpoint or a pickup works.
    CanCollide: boolean
    --- Whether this part takes part in touch events. With it off, neither `Touched` nor `TouchEnded`
    --- fires for this part or for a part that touches it. Collisions are still resolved. Turn it off
    --- for scenery that no script listens to.
    CanTouch: boolean
    --- Whether raycasts, shapecasts and overlap queries can find this part. It is independent of
    --- `CanCollide`: a part can block movement and still be ignored by a camera ray.
    CanQuery: boolean
    --- Whether a `Water` pushes this part up. An unanchored part in water is lifted by the water it
    --- displaces, at several points across its shape, so a hull pitches and rolls; it is also slowed by
    --- the water's `Viscosity`. With it off, water ignores the part.
    Buoyant: boolean
    --- The name of the collision group that decides which parts this one collides with. Register groups
    --- with `PhysicsService:RegisterCollisionGroup`. Setting a name that is not registered raises an
    --- error.
    CollisionGroup: string
    --- How much this surface resists sliding, from 0 (no friction) to 1 (very grippy). A contact uses a
    --- combination of both surfaces' values, so one slippery part is enough to make a pair slide.
    Friction: number
    --- How much this surface bounces, from 0 (no bounce) to 1 (a perfect bounce). A contact combines
    --- both surfaces' values, as it does for `Friction`.
    Restitution: number
    --- The mass of this part per cubic meter, in kilograms. The part's `Mass` is its density times the
    --- volume of its shape.
    Density: number
    --- The mass of this part in kilograms: `Density` times the volume of its shape. Change `Density` or
    --- `Size` to change it.
    read Mass: number
    --- The total mass, in kilograms, of this part and every part rigidly joined to it by a `Weld` or a
    --- `FixedConstraint`.
    read AssemblyMass: number
    --- How quickly this part slows down when nothing touches it, as the share of its speed that it
    --- loses each second. At 0 it never slows; 1 feels like moving through thick air.
    LinearDamping: number
    --- How quickly this part stops spinning when nothing touches it, as the share of its spin that it
    --- loses each second.
    AngularDamping: number
    --- How much a round part resists rolling while it touches something: it slows steadily, by this
    --- many times the strength of gravity, until it stops. 0.01 is a hard ball on wood, 0.001 a steel
    --- ball on steel and 0.05 a ball on carpet. A ball, a cylinder and a capsule are slowed by it, and
    --- a part of another shape is not. 0 turns it off.
    RollingResistance: number
    --- Whether `Collided` fires for this part. Off by default, because reporting every contact has a
    --- cost.
    ContactDetails: boolean
    --- The velocity of this part in meters per second, as of the last simulation tick. Setting it sets
    --- the velocity for the next tick. To push the part instead of setting its velocity, use
    --- `ApplyImpulse`.
    LinearVelocity: vector
    --- The angular velocity of this part in radians per second about each world axis, as of the last
    --- simulation tick. Setting it sets the spin for the next tick.
    AngularVelocity: vector
    --- Fires when another part starts touching this one. Passes the other part. A pair that stays in
    --- contact fires once, not every tick. Like every signal, it is deferred: handlers run at the next
    --- resumption point, not inside the physics step.
    read Touched: Signal<BasePart>
    --- Fires together with `Touched` when `ContactDetails` is on, with details of the contact. Passes
    --- the other part, the contact point in world space, the direction from this part into the other,
    --- and the speed at which the two were approaching along that direction, in meters per second,
    --- before the collision was resolved.
    read Collided: Signal<BasePart, vector, vector, number>
    --- Fires when another part stops touching this one. Passes the other part. It does not fire for
    --- contacts that end because a part was destroyed.
    read TouchEnded: Signal<BasePart>
    --- Overrides one parameter of this part's material, for this part only. Raises an error if the
    --- material does not declare the parameter. `Color` and `Emissive` take a `Color3`; the other
    --- built-in parameters take a number. A parameter of the material's surface shader can be set if
    --- the material lists it in `instanceParameters`. It takes a number, a boolean, a `Vector2`, a
    --- `vector`, a `Color3`, or a table of four numbers, but not a texture. Surface shader parameters
    --- are not replicated: set them on the machine that draws the part.
    function SetMaterialParameter(self, name: string, value: Color3 | number | boolean | Vector2 | vector | { number }): ()
    --- Returns the value this part is drawn with for one material parameter: the part's override if it
    --- has one, otherwise the material's own value. Returns nil for a surface shader parameter that
    --- neither sets; the shader's default is then used.
    function GetMaterialParameter(self, name: string): (Color3 | number | Vector2 | vector | { number })?
    --- Removes this part's override of one material parameter, so the part uses the material's value
    --- again. Does nothing if the parameter is not overridden.
    function ClearMaterialParameter(self, name: string): ()
    --- Gives one player's machine the simulation of this part. That machine simulates the part and
    --- sends its position, so what the player pushes moves immediately on their screen. Pass nil to
    --- return the part to the authority. Only the authority can call it, and only on an unanchored
    --- part. When a player leaves, the parts they owned return to the authority. The owner's results
    --- are trusted: validate them, or do not hand the part over, if cheating matters.
    function SetNetworkOwner(self, player: Player?): ()
    --- Returns the player whose machine simulates this part, or nil if the authority does. On a client
    --- it returns the local `Player` for a part that client owns, and nil for every other part.
    function GetNetworkOwner(self): Player?
    --- Applies an instant push at the part's center of mass, in kilogram meters per second. It takes
    --- effect at the next simulation tick. An anchored part ignores it.
    function ApplyImpulse(self, impulse: vector): ()
    --- Breaks the part into pieces and returns them in a `Model` where the part was. The part is
    --- destroyed. Each piece is a part like the original, held to the pieces beside it by a
    --- `FixedConstraint` that breaks under `Strength` newtons, so the pieces stand together until
    --- something hits them hard enough. The pieces are unanchored. A part that was anchored keeps its
    --- footing: an anchored `Footing` part under its bottom, which does not collide, holds the pieces
    --- along the bottom by the same joints, so a wall breaks where it is hit rather than falling over
    --- whole. `options.Pieces` is how many, from 1 to 64 (8 by default). `options.Seed` chooses the
    --- cut: the same seed and the same part give the same pieces on every machine. `options.Strength`
    --- is what each joint holds; by default it is twice the part's weight. The pieces are simulation:
    --- in a match the server's, sent to every player as parts, so use as few as the moment needs. Dust
    --- and small chips are better as a `ParticleEmitter` burst when a joint breaks: drawn on each
    --- machine and sent as nothing but the burst. Only a `Part` whose `Shape` is `Block` can be broken.
    function Fracture(self, options: { Pieces: number?, Seed: number?, Strength: number? }?): Model
    --- Runs `fn(character, step)` inside the simulation step when a player's character that this
    --- machine simulates starts touching this part. Use it for a jump pad, a booster or a trampoline,
    --- so the effect is predicted on the player's machine and needs no correction. On a client the
    --- function runs again for each tick that is replayed after a correction, as a function bound with
    --- `RunService:BindToPredictedStep` does. A part has one such function; binding again replaces it.
    --- The function must not yield.
    function BindToPredictedTouch(self, fn: (character: BasePart, step: PredictedStep) -> ()): ()
    --- Removes the function bound with `BindToPredictedTouch`. Does nothing if none is bound.
    function UnbindFromPredictedTouch(self): ()
    --- Returns where this part is drawn in the current frame, which is between its positions at the
    --- last two simulation ticks. Use it for anything that must line up with the picture, such as a
    --- camera that follows the part. It differs from `CFrame` only inside a render step: a function
    --- bound with `RunService:BindToRenderStep`, a `PreRender` handler, or an action whose context has
    --- `Rate = Render`. Everywhere else, including on a server, it returns `CFrame`, so simulation code
    --- never depends on frame timing.
    function GetRenderCFrame(self): CFrame
    --- Applies an instant push at a point in world space, in kilogram meters per second. A push away
    --- from the center of mass also turns the part. It takes effect at the next simulation tick.
    function ApplyImpulseAtPosition(self, impulse: vector, position: vector): ()
    --- Applies an instant twist about the axis the vector points along, in kilogram square meters per
    --- second. It changes the part's spin without moving it. It takes effect at the next simulation
    --- tick.
    function ApplyAngularImpulse(self, impulse: vector): ()
end

declare extern type Part extends BasePart with
    --- Which built-in solid this part is. The shape sets both what is drawn and what collides. `Size`
    --- is the full extent of the shape. A `Ball` is a sphere whose diameter is the largest of the three
    --- sizes. A `Cylinder` and a `Capsule` stand along the Y axis: their diameter is the larger of X
    --- and Z, and their height, including the caps, is Y. A capsule's drawn shape matches its collision
    --- shape exactly when `Size.y` is twice `Size.x`. A `Wedge` is a ramp whose tall face points toward
    --- -Z. It collides as its whole box, not as a ramp.
    Shape: EnumPartShape
end

declare extern type MeshPart extends BasePart with
    --- The model file this part draws, as an `asset://` path to a glTF file. Setting it replaces the
    --- mesh. If the file fails to load, the part keeps its previous mesh and the error is logged.
    MeshContent: Content
    --- How closely the collision shape follows the mesh. `Box` collides as the mesh's bounding box.
    --- Every other value collides as the mesh's convex hull. Until the mesh has loaded, the part
    --- collides as its bounding box. A mesh too thin or too small to form a solid also collides as its
    --- bounding box. The property reads back the value you set.
    CollisionFidelity: EnumCollisionFidelity
    --- The size of the mesh as it was authored, in meters. The part is scaled by `Size` divided by
    --- `MeshSize`, so setting `Size` to twice `MeshSize` draws and collides the mesh at twice its
    --- authored size. The importer sets this value and it is saved with the scene. You can set it
    --- yourself to stretch the mesh. Each component must be greater than 0.
    MeshSize: vector
    --- Another skinned `MeshPart` whose pose this one copies. Set it on a piece of armor, hair, a glove
    --- or a held item so that the piece moves with the body it is worn on. Every joint that both meshes
    --- have follows the leader's joint exactly, whatever moved it: a clip, an animation graph, an
    --- `IKControl`, a `SpringBone` or a ragdoll. Joints are matched by name. A joint that only this
    --- mesh has stays at rest relative to its parent, unless an animation playing on the leader has a
    --- track for it. A mesh that follows a leader has no physics body of its own: it does not collide,
    --- and raycasts pass through it. It keeps its own `SpringBone` chains and morph targets. It follows
    --- nothing if it names itself, if the leaders form a loop, or if the leader has no skeleton. To
    --- attach an item that shares no joints with the body, parent it under a `Bone` of the body
    --- instead.
    PoseFrom: MeshPart?
    --- Returns the names of this mesh's morph targets (shape keys), in the order the file lists them.
    --- Returns an empty array if the mesh has none, or if its file has not loaded yet. To be sure the
    --- mesh is loaded, call `ContentProvider:PreloadAsync` first.
    function GetMorphTargets(self): { string }
    --- Sets the weight of one morph target of this mesh. 0 is the mesh at rest and 1 is the full shape.
    --- Values outside that range are allowed, and several targets add together. The weight applies only
    --- on the machine where the script runs. It is not replicated and not recorded in a replay, so set
    --- it in a client script. It overrides what a playing clip sets for the same target until you call
    --- `ClearMorphWeight`. Raises an error for a name the mesh does not have, once the mesh has loaded.
    --- A weight set before the mesh loads is kept and applied when it arrives.
    function SetMorphWeight(self, name: string, weight: number): ()
    --- Returns the weight this part is drawn with for one morph target, on this machine. That is the
    --- value a script set, or else the value from the clips playing on the mesh, or else the weight the
    --- file gives at rest. Returns 0 for a name the mesh does not have.
    function GetMorphWeight(self, name: string): number
    --- Removes the weight that `SetMorphWeight` set for one morph target. From the next frame, the
    --- target follows the playing clips again, or the weight the file gives at rest.
    function ClearMorphWeight(self, name: string): ()
end

declare extern type Attachment extends Instance with
    --- The position and orientation of the attachment relative to the part it is parented to.
    CFrame: CFrame
    --- The position and orientation of the attachment in world space. It is updated every tick as the
    --- part moves.
    read WorldCFrame: CFrame
    --- Returns where this attachment is drawn in the current frame, which is between its positions at
    --- the last two simulation ticks. Use it for anything that must line up with the picture, such as a
    --- camera that follows the attachment. It differs from `WorldCFrame` only inside a render step: a
    --- function bound with `RunService:BindToRenderStep`, a `PreRender` handler, or an action whose
    --- context has `Rate = Render`. Everywhere else, including on a server, it returns `WorldCFrame`.
    function GetRenderCFrame(self): CFrame
end

declare extern type Bone extends Attachment with
    --- The name of the joint this bone follows, as it is named in the model file of the parent
    --- `MeshPart`.
    JointName: string
    --- The index of the joint that `JointName` matched, or -1 if the rig has no joint of that name. It
    --- is looked up again when the mesh reloads.
    read JointIndex: number
    --- An offset added to the joint's animated pose, in the joint's own space. Use it for procedural
    --- animation on top of a clip: bend an arm toward a target, turn a head, kick a weapon back on a
    --- shot. The identity, the default, leaves the pose unchanged.
    Transform: CFrame
end

declare extern type IKControl extends Instance with
    --- What the control does. `TwoBone` treats the end joint and the two joints above it as a limb and
    --- moves the end to the target. `LookAt` turns the end joint to face the target; the joints above
    --- it share the turn.
    Type: EnumIKControlType
    --- The name of the joint at the end of the limb, as it is named in the model file: a hand, a foot
    --- or a head. The control does nothing if the rig has no joint of that name.
    EndJoint: string
    --- The part or attachment to reach for or look at. The control uses the position where the target
    --- is drawn in the current frame. With no target, the control does nothing.
    Target: (BasePart | Attachment)?
    --- An offset from the target, in the target's own space. Use it to aim at a point on the target,
    --- such as a grip a hand's width from the middle of a weapon.
    TargetOffset: CFrame
    --- For `TwoBone`: the part or attachment the elbow or knee points toward. With none, the limb bends
    --- the way the animation already bends it.
    Pole: (BasePart | Attachment)?
    --- For `TwoBone`: whether the end joint also takes the target's rotation, so a palm lies flat on a
    --- grip or a sole lies flat on a pedal.
    AlignRotation: boolean
    --- For `LookAt`: how many joints above the end joint share the turn, from 0 to 8. With 0 only the
    --- head turns; with 2, the head, the neck and one spine joint each take an equal share.
    ChainLength: number
    --- For `LookAt`: the largest angle, in degrees, that the look can turn away from straight ahead.
    --- Beyond it, the look stops at the limit.
    MaxAngle: number
    --- How strongly the control is applied, from 0 to 1. At 0 only the animation shows. Tween it to
    --- blend the control in and out smoothly.
    Weight: number
    --- Whether the control is applied. A disabled control costs nothing.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- The time, in seconds, over which a sudden change is eased, such as a target that jumps or the
    --- control being enabled. At 0 the control follows immediately.
    Smoothing: number
end

declare extern type FootPlacement extends Instance with
    --- The name of the left ankle joint, as it is named in the model file.
    LeftFoot: string
    --- The name of the right ankle joint, as it is named in the model file.
    RightFoot: string
    --- The name of the joint both legs hang from. It is lowered when one foot is below the ground level
    --- the animation assumes.
    Hips: string
    --- The distance from the ankle joint down to the sole, in meters. The ankle is kept this far above
    --- the ground.
    FootHeight: number
    --- The largest distance, in meters, that a foot is moved up or down from where the animation put
    --- it. Ground farther away than that, such as a ledge or a drop, is ignored.
    StepHeight: number
    --- Whether a planted foot is rotated to lie flat on the ground under it.
    AlignToSlope: boolean
    --- How strongly the placement is applied, from 0 to 1.
    Weight: number
    --- Whether the placement is applied.
    Enabled: boolean
end

declare extern type LipSync extends Instance with
    --- The instance whose voice moves the mouth: a `Voice` sound that is this instance, or is under it,
    --- drives the face. With nil, the source is the parent of the `MeshPart`.
    Source: Instance?
    --- Which method is used. `Auto`, the default, plays a viseme track when the line has one and
    --- otherwise falls back to analyzing the sound.
    Mode: EnumLipSyncMode
    --- The face map file, as an `asset://` path ending in `.face.json`. It says which morph targets
    --- each viseme, frequency band and loudness level moves. When empty, the file beside the model is
    --- used: the model's path with its extension replaced by `.face.json`.
    Map: Content
    --- The time, in seconds, a morph target takes to reach a new weight. At 0 the mouth snaps between
    --- shapes; 0.1 is a slow mouth.
    Smoothing: number
    --- How strongly the mouth moves, from 0 to 2. Use 0.5 for a mumble and more than 1 for a shout.
    Weight: number
    --- Whether the mouth is moved.
    Enabled: boolean
end

declare extern type SpringBone extends Instance with
    --- The name of the joint the chain hangs from, as it is named in the model file. That joint stays
    --- where the animation puts it; every joint below it is moved. Nothing moves if the rig has no
    --- joint of that name.
    RootJoint: string
    --- A name pattern for the top joint of each chain, where `*` matches any run of characters. Every
    --- joint the pattern matches starts its own chain: `Cape_*0` matches the top of each column of a
    --- cape whose joints are named `Cape_L0`, `Cape_L1`, and so on. A matched joint that is below
    --- another matched joint belongs to that joint's chain. Use it with `RootJoint` or in place of it.
    JointPattern: string
    --- Whether the chain is moved. When it is off, the joints return to their animated pose from the
    --- next frame.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- How strongly each joint is pulled back toward its animated pose, from 0 to 1. At 0 the chain
    --- hangs like a rope; at 1 it does not move.
    Stiffness: number
    --- How much of its motion a joint loses each step, from 0 to 1. A low value swings for a long time;
    --- a high value settles quickly and looks heavy.
    Damping: number
    --- How much of the world's gravity affects the chain. At 0 the chain floats; a negative value makes
    --- it rise.
    GravityScale: number
    --- How much the chain lags behind the body's motion, from 0 to 1. At 1 a cape streams out behind a
    --- running character; at 0 it moves with the body as if there were no air.
    Inertia: number
    --- The largest angle, in degrees, that a joint can bend away from its animated direction.
    LimitAngle: number
    --- The thickness of the chain where it meets a `SpringCollider`, in the mesh's units.
    Radius: number
    --- How much the world's wind pushes the chain. At 0 the chain ignores wind.
    WindInfluence: number
end

declare extern type SpringCollider extends Instance with
    --- The name of the joint the collider follows, as it is named in the model file. If the rig has no
    --- joint of that name, the collider follows the `MeshPart` itself.
    JointName: string
    --- The radius of the sphere, or of the capsule's ends.
    Radius: number
    --- The length of the capsule along the joint's up axis, starting at `Offset`. At 0 the collider is
    --- a sphere.
    Length: number
    --- The position of the sphere's center, or of the capsule's lower end, in the joint's own space.
    Offset: vector
end

declare extern type Cloth extends Instance with
    --- Whether the cloth is simulated. When it is off, the mesh is drawn as the animation has it.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- A name pattern for the joints the cloth hangs from, where `*` matches any run of characters. A
    --- vertex weighted at least half to joints it matches is pinned to where the animation puts it.
    --- With no pinned vertex, the cloth hangs only by `MaxDistance`.
    PinJoints: string
    --- How far, in meters, a vertex may move from where the animation puts it. A cape that should never
    --- fly over the head has a small one; at 0 the cloth does not move.
    MaxDistance: number
    --- How much the cloth resists stretching, from 0 to 1. At 1 it keeps the length it was modeled
    --- with, as canvas does; lower values stretch like knitted cloth.
    Stiffness: number
    --- How much the cloth resists folding, from 0 to 1. Silk is near 0 and leather near 1.
    BendStiffness: number
    --- How much of its motion the cloth loses each second, from 0 to 1. A low value flutters for a long
    --- time; a high value settles quickly and looks heavy.
    Damping: number
    --- How much of the world's gravity affects the cloth. At 0 the cloth floats.
    GravityScale: number
    --- How much the world's wind pushes the cloth. At 0 the cloth ignores wind.
    WindInfluence: number
    --- How far, in meters, the cloth stays from what it rests on.
    Thickness: number
    --- Whether the cloth is kept out of the anchored parts near it, such as a wall it is pressed
    --- against or the floor it drags on. It never pushes them.
    CollidesWithWorld: boolean
end

declare extern type Constraint extends Instance with
    --- The attachment at one end of the joint. The part it is on is one of the two bodies the joint
    --- connects.
    Attachment0: Attachment?
    --- The attachment at the other end of the joint. When the joint is created, the two attachments
    --- mark the same place on the two parts.
    Attachment1: Attachment?
    --- Whether the joint holds. A disabled constraint stays in the world and holds nothing. It keeps
    --- its place in the solver's order, so enabling it again behaves as it did before; destroying and
    --- recreating a joint does not guarantee that.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- Whether the two connected parts collide with each other. Turn it off for parts that overlap at
    --- the joint, such as the upper and lower arm of a ragdoll, or they push each other apart every
    --- step.
    CollideConnected: boolean
    --- Whether the constraint is drawn. In the editor and in the debug overlay, every constraint is
    --- drawn as a shape that shows what it is. In a running game, only a rope, a rod and a spring are
    --- drawn, as a thin line with their `Color` and `Thickness`.
    Visible: boolean
    --- The force, in newtons, above which the joint breaks. A joint that breaks is disabled and fires
    --- `Broken`. At 0 the joint never breaks.
    BreakForce: number
    --- The torque, in newton meters, above which the joint breaks. At 0 the joint never breaks.
    BreakTorque: number
    --- Fires when the joint breaks because it carried more than `BreakForce` or `BreakTorque`. The
    --- joint is disabled, not destroyed; set `Enabled` to true to restore it.
    read Broken: Signal<()>
    --- Returns the force, in newtons, that the joint applied during the last simulation tick to hold
    --- its two parts together. This is the value `BreakForce` is compared with. Returns 0 for a
    --- constraint that only drives motion, such as `AlignPosition`; use `GetMotorForce` for those.
    function GetForce(self): number
    --- Returns the torque, in newton meters, that the joint applied during the last simulation tick to
    --- resist turning. Returns 0 for a joint that does not resist turning, such as a ball joint or a
    --- rope.
    function GetTorque(self): number
    --- Returns the force, in newtons, that the constraint's motor applied during the last simulation
    --- tick. It applies to the actuator of a `PrismaticConstraint`, an `AlignPosition` and a
    --- `LinearVelocity`. It is separate from `GetForce`, which is the load the joint carried. Returns 0
    --- for a constraint with no linear motor.
    function GetMotorForce(self): number
    --- Returns the torque, in newton meters, that the constraint's motor applied during the last
    --- simulation tick. It applies to the actuator of a `HingeConstraint` or a `BallSocketConstraint`,
    --- an `AlignOrientation` and an `AngularVelocity`. A servo that reads its `ServoMaxTorque` here is
    --- at its limit. Returns 0 for a constraint with no rotary motor.
    function GetMotorTorque(self): number
end

declare extern type BallSocketConstraint extends Constraint with
    --- Whether `UpperAngle` and `TwistLimit` apply. When it is off, the joint rotates freely.
    LimitsEnabled: boolean
    --- The half-angle of the cone the joint can swing within, in degrees, measured from the X axis of
    --- `Attachment0`.
    UpperAngle: number
    --- How far the joint can twist about its X axis, in degrees, in either direction.
    TwistLimit: number
    --- Whether the joint drives itself. `None` does nothing; `Servo` holds the orientation set in
    --- `TargetOrientation`. `Motor` is treated as `None`, because a ball joint has no single axis to
    --- spin about.
    ActuatorType: EnumActuatorType
    --- For `Servo`: the orientation of `Attachment1` relative to `Attachment0` that the joint holds.
    --- Only the rotation is used. The identity is the pose the two parts were joined in. With the
    --- joint's X axis along a limb, a rotation about X twists the limb, and a rotation about Y or Z
    --- swings it.
    TargetOrientation: CFrame
    --- For `Servo`: the largest torque the joint can apply, in newton meters, about each of its axes. A
    --- load that needs more than this pulls the joint away from its target.
    ServoMaxTorque: number
    --- For `Servo`: how quickly the joint turns toward its target, as a frequency in radians per
    --- second. Used only when `Stiffness` is 0. The joint settles without overshooting. The value is
