declare extern type Terrain extends Instance with
    --- The size of one voxel, in meters, from 0.1 to 64. One meter resolves a doorway or a path. Two
    --- meters resolves a hillside and uses an eighth of the memory. It can be set only while the
    --- terrain is empty. Call `Clear` first to change it on a sculpted terrain.
    VoxelSize: number
    --- The width of one streamed cell of terrain, in meters. It is the spacing of the world's streaming
    --- grid. A terrain saved with a scene that spans 256 cells or more is divided into cells of this
    --- size, rounded to whole columns of 32-voxel chunks. Each cell is loaded and unloaded around the
    --- focuses of `StreamingService`, at its terrain radii. A cell that was changed during a run is
    --- written to the run's cache on disk when it is unloaded, and loads again with the change.
    read CellSize: number
    --- The position of the terrain's origin in the world, in meters. Every voxel, collider and edit is
    --- offset by it, so moving a sculpted terrain is cheap. A terrain can be moved but not rotated.
    Position: vector
    --- The lowest height, in meters, at which a voxel can be written. Ground created where there was
    --- none, by `WriteHeights`, by the editor's Generate Flat Ground or by raising empty terrain, is a
    --- slab 32 meters deep below its lowest surface, rounded down to a whole chunk and never below this
    --- height.
    MinHeight: number
    --- The highest height, in meters, at which a voxel can be written.
    MaxHeight: number
    --- The number of 32 by 32 by 32 voxel chunks that currently hold any ground. Air is not stored, so
    --- the count is 0 for an empty terrain.
    read CellCount: number
    --- Returns whether the ground within `radius` of `center` is drawn in its final form. That means
    --- every piece the view needs there has its mesh built and uploaded at the detail it will be drawn
    --- at, and no further refinement is pending. Ground that a script has just written exists at once
    --- and is meshed a few frames later; this method reports the second. The answer is as of the last
    --- frame drawn. It is always true where nothing is drawn, such as on a server or in a run with no
    --- camera.
    function IsMeshed(self, center: vector, radius: number): boolean
    --- Yields until `IsMeshed(center, radius)` is true and returns true, or returns false after
    --- `timeout` seconds of simulation time. Use it in a loading screen after building a terrain,
    --- before letting the player in. Together with `SceneService:HoldLoading`, it keeps the engine's
    --- loading screen up until the ground is ready.
    function WaitForMeshAsync(self, center: vector, radius: number, timeout: number?): boolean
    --- Adds a ball of ground, or removes one when `material` is 0. Returns the number of voxels it
    --- changed. Adding never removes ground, and removing never adds it. A voxel ends up as full as the
    --- fuller of itself and the ball when adding, or as empty as the ball leaves it when removing. An
    --- explosion crater is `FillBall(hit.Position, 4, 0)`. Raises an error for a position or size that
    --- is not a finite number, or for a negative radius. This applies to every `Terrain` method that
    --- edits or reads. A return value of 0 means the ground was already as requested, or the edit was
    --- entirely outside `MinHeight` to `MaxHeight`.
    function FillBall(self, center: vector, radius: number, material: number): number
    --- Adds an axis-aligned box of ground `size` across, or removes one when `material` is 0. Returns
    --- the number of voxels it changed.
    function FillBlock(self, center: vector, size: vector, material: number): number
    --- Adds an upright cylinder of ground `height` tall, or removes one when `material` is 0. Returns
    --- the number of voxels it changed. A well is `FillCylinder(top - vector.create(0, 10, 0), 20, 1.5,
    --- 0)`.
    function FillCylinder(self, center: vector, height: number, radius: number, material: number): number
    --- Raises the ground around `center` by `amount` meters at the middle, falling smoothly to nothing
    --- at `radius`. A negative `amount` lowers it. Returns the number of voxels it changed. It moves
    --- the existing surface instead of adding a ball. The ground within `radius` above and below
    --- `center` is pushed up, so a hill rises with no overhang at its rim, and a tunnel farther down
    --- stays where it is. Use it to shape hills and valleys; use `FillBall` for a boulder or a tunnel.
    --- Where a column within reach has no ground and `material` is given, raising creates new ground of
    --- that material from the height of `center` upward.
    function RaiseBall(self, center: vector, radius: number, amount: number, material: number?): number
    --- Moves the surface near `center` outward along its own slope by `amount` meters at the middle,
    --- falling smoothly to nothing at `radius`. A negative `amount` moves it inward. Returns the number
    --- of voxels it changed. This is what the editor's Raise and Lower tools do: a field rises, a cliff
    --- moves forward, and an overhang grows downward. New ground is made of `material`, or of the
    --- neighboring ground's material when none is given.
    function GrowBall(self, center: vector, radius: number, amount: number, material: number?): number
    --- Smooths the ground in a ball. Each point of the surface moves toward the average of the surface
    --- around it by `strength` (0 to 1, default 0.5), less toward the rim. Returns the number of voxels
    --- it changed. Flat ground does not move, and a slab is never cut through. Material removed from a
    --- bump is spread around it, so the volume of ground stays nearly constant over repeated strokes.
    --- The average is taken over a width of half the brush, at most four voxels.
    function SmoothBall(self, center: vector, radius: number, strength: number?): number
    --- Pulls the ground in a ball toward a level plane at `height`, removing what is above the plane
    --- and filling what is below it. `strength` (0 to 1, default 1) sets how far, less toward the rim.
    --- Returns the number of voxels it changed.
    function FlattenBall(self, center: vector, radius: number, height: number, strength: number?): number
    --- Changes the material of the ground in a ball without moving the surface. Returns the number of
    --- voxels it changed. A voxel holds two materials, one over the other, and how much of the upper
    --- one shows. `options.Mode` chooses what the stroke does with them: - `Replace`, the default,
    --- makes the ground `material` outright. - `Blend` shows more of `material` over what is there, by
    --- `Strength` (0 to 1, default 1). `Falloff` is the share of the radius over which the stroke fades
    --- toward the rim: 0, the default, is a hard edge, 0.5 fades over the outer half, and 1 fades from
    --- the center. - `Under` puts `material` under what was painted over it. - `Erase` shows less of
    --- the upper material, revealing the one under it. A `material` of 0 raises an error, except in
    --- `Erase` mode, which takes no material.
    function PaintBall(self, center: vector, radius: number, material: number, options: { Mode: EnumTerrainPaintMode?, Strength: number?, Falloff: number? }?): number
    --- Changes every voxel of material `from` to material `to` in the box between two corners. Returns
    --- the number of voxels it changed. Raises an error if either material is 0: this method changes
    --- what the ground is made of, not whether it is there.
    function ReplaceMaterial(self, minCorner: vector, maxCorner: vector, from: number, to: number): number
    --- Returns the voxels in the box between two corners, snapped outward to the grid. The results are
    --- their materials, their occupancies (0 to 1) and the number of voxels along each axis. The two
    --- tables are flat. The voxel at `x, y, z`, counted from 0 at the low corner, is at index `1 + x +
    --- size.X * (y + size.Y * z)`. One call reads at most 256 voxels along each axis; read a larger
    --- region in pieces.
    function ReadVoxels(self, minCorner: vector, maxCorner: vector): ({ number }, { number }, vector)
    --- Writes a box of voxels in one call, in the layout that `ReadVoxels` returns. `corner` is a point
    --- in the first voxel, `size` is the number of voxels along each axis, and each table holds one
    --- entry per voxel. Returns the number of voxels it changed. A material of 0 or an occupancy of 0
    --- is air. Writing what `ReadVoxels` returned restores the terrain exactly. Use it to copy a piece
    --- of terrain, to generate terrain voxel by voxel, or to undo a script's own change.
    function WriteVoxels(self, corner: vector, size: vector, materials: { number }, occupancies: { number }): number
    --- Returns the voxel that a world position falls in, as its integer index along each axis.
    function WorldToCell(self, position: vector): vector
    --- Returns the world position of the center of a voxel, given its index along each axis.
    function CellCenterToWorld(self, cell: vector): vector
    --- Returns the height of the top of the ground at a position, in meters, or nil where there is no
    --- ground. The result is the highest surface in the column, even above a cave. That is where to
    --- plant a tree or where a player lands from above. For the first surface along a direction, use
    --- `Workspace:Raycast`. It also returns nil where the ground has not streamed in yet. In a large
    --- world, wait for the area with `StreamingService:LoadAreaAsync(position, radius)` first.
    function HeightAt(self, x: number, z: number): number?
    --- Writes a heightmap in one call: one height per column of voxels, `columns` to a row, row after
    --- row along +Z, starting at the column that `corner` falls in. Only the X and Z of `corner` are
    --- used. Returns the number of voxels it changed. Use it for ground that comes from data, such as
    --- generated noise or an image, where one brush call per column would be far too slow. Heights are
    --- in meters in world space and are clamped between `MinHeight` and `MaxHeight`. A height that is
    --- not a number is skipped. `material` (default 1) is what new ground is made of: one id for all of
    --- it, or a table of ids parallel to `heights`, in which 0 leaves that column unchanged. The top of
    --- each column moves to its height: ground is added from the old top upward or removed from it
    --- downward, and a cave below the top is kept. A column with no ground at all is created as a slab
    --- 32 meters deep below the lowest height in the table.
    function WriteHeights(self, corner: vector, columns: number, heights: { number }, material: (number | { number })?): number
    --- Removes every voxel. The terrain is then empty and `VoxelSize` can be set again.
    function Clear(self): ()
    --- Returns the terrain's layers: a list of material URNs in which entry `n` is the material that
    --- voxels of material `n` are drawn with. A new terrain has none. A scene saved by a version of the
    --- engine in which terrains had built-in layers returns those eight, `engine://terrain/grass` to
    --- `engine://terrain/ice`.
    function GetLayers(self): { string }
    --- Sets the terrain's layers, in the form that `GetLayers` returns: up to 255 material URNs, entry
    --- `n` for material `n`. Voxels keep their numbers, so replacing entry 3 changes every voxel of
    --- material 3 at once. To paint with a new material, add it at the end. Layers are saved with the
    --- scene.
    function SetLayers(self, layers: { string }): ()
    --- Returns the rules that choose the ground's material by slope and height, in the order they are
    --- applied. A new terrain has none. Each rule draws `Material` where the ground is between
    --- `SlopeMin` and `SlopeMax` degrees and between `HeightMin` and `HeightMax` meters. `AppliesTo`
    --- lists the layers it draws over; an empty list means every layer except its own. `Blend` is the
    --- width of its edge, in degrees or meters, and `Noise` is how irregular the edge is. Rules change
    --- what is drawn, not the voxels: the voxels keep their materials until `ApplyRules`. A raycast
    --- reports the material that is drawn.
    function GetRules(self): { { Enabled: boolean, Material: number, SlopeMin: number, SlopeMax: number, HeightMin: number, HeightMax: number, Blend: number, Noise: number, AppliesTo: { number } } }
    --- Sets the terrain's rules, in the form that `GetRules` returns: up to 16, applied in order. A
    --- field left out takes its default: `Enabled` true, slopes 0 to 90, no height limits, `Blend` 10
    --- and `Noise` 0.12. An empty list removes all rules. Rules are saved with the scene.
    function SetRules(self, rules: { { read Enabled: boolean?, read Material: number?, read SlopeMin: number?, read SlopeMax: number?, read HeightMin: number?, read HeightMax: number?, read Blend: number?, read Noise: number?, read AppliesTo: { number }? } }): ()
    --- Writes the materials that the rules draw into the surface voxels in the box between two corners.
    --- The rules can then be removed with no visible change. Returns the number of voxels it changed.
    function ApplyRules(self, minCorner: vector, maxCorner: vector): number
    --- Does nothing and returns 0. Terrain storage is always compact. The method is kept so that
    --- scripts that call it still run.
    function Compact(self): number
end

declare extern type WeldConstraint extends Instance with
    --- The part that `Part1` follows.
    Part0: BasePart?
    --- The part that follows `Part0`.
    Part1: BasePart?
    --- Whether the weld holds. Each time it changes from false to true, the offset between the parts is
    --- captured again. To weld a part somewhere else, disable the weld, move the part and enable it
    --- again.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- Whether the weld is currently holding: it is enabled, both parts are set, and both are in the
    --- world. A weld with one part missing is not an error; it is inactive until the other part is set.
    read Active: boolean
end

declare extern type CharacterBody extends BasePart with
    --- The speed at which `Move` moves the character along the ground, in meters per second. The
    --- direction given to `Move` is multiplied by this, so a direction of length 0.5 walks at half
    --- speed.
    WalkSpeed: number
    --- The upward speed that `Jump` gives the character, in meters per second. The height of the jump
    --- depends on `Workspace.Gravity`. A negative value launches the character downward.
    JumpSpeed: number
    --- How much of `Workspace.Gravity` affects the character. 1 is all of it, 0.5 gives a floaty jump,
    --- and 0 is none. With a negative value the character falls upward.
    GravityScale: number
    --- The speed at which `Move` moves the character through a fluid, in meters per second.
    SwimSpeed: number
    --- The speed at which `Move` moves the character while `Flying` is on, in meters per second.
    FlySpeed: number
    --- Whether the character flies: gravity does not affect it, and `Move` uses all three axes at
    --- `FlySpeed`. Use it for a creative mode, a ghost or a jetpack.
    Flying: boolean
    --- The steepest ground the character can stand and walk on, in degrees from horizontal. On steeper
    --- ground it slides down.
    MaxSlopeAngle: number
    --- The height of the tallest ledge the character steps over instead of being stopped by, in meters.
    --- At 0, a curb stops it.
    AutoStepHeight: number
    --- Whether the character was standing on walkable ground at the last simulation tick. It is true
    --- exactly when `State` is `Grounded`.
    read Grounded: boolean
    --- The material the character is standing on: the material of the part under it, or the terrain
    --- layer drawn under its feet. It is what `RaycastResult.Material` reports for a ray cast straight
    --- down. It is nil in the air, in water and on a part with no material. Read its `FootstepSound`
    --- for a footstep, and its `Friction` or `Tags` to treat ice or mud differently.
    read FloorMaterial: Material?
    --- What the character is doing. - `Grounded`: standing on walkable ground. - `Airborne`: not
    --- supported. Ground too steep to walk on also reads as `Airborne`. - `Swimming`: its middle is
    --- below the surface of a `Water` or inside a fluid block. The character enters and leaves this
    --- state by itself. - `Flying`: `Flying` is on.
    read State: EnumCharacterState
    --- Fires when the character becomes `Grounded` after being `Airborne`. Passes the part it landed
    --- on, or nil when it landed on terrain.
    read Landed: Signal<BasePart?>
    --- Sets the direction the character moves in during the next simulation tick, in world space. Call
    --- it every tick while the character should move; a character that is not told to move stops. On
    --- foot, only the horizontal part of the direction is used; gravity and `Jump` control the vertical
    --- motion. The direction is multiplied by `WalkSpeed` and is not normalized, so a shorter vector
    --- walks more slowly. While swimming or flying, all three axes are used, at `SwimSpeed` or
    --- `FlySpeed`. A character can be pushed like a part. `ApplyImpulse` changes its velocity by the
    --- impulse divided by its `Mass`, and `LinearVelocity` can be set, for a knockback or a launch pad.
    --- The push fades, quickly on the ground and slowly in the air, and the walk is added to it.
    function Move(self, direction: vector): ()
    --- Launches the character upward at `JumpSpeed` at the next simulation tick. It does not check
    --- whether the character is on the ground, so that a double jump or a wall jump can be written with
    --- the same call. For an ordinary jump, check first: `if character.Grounded then character:Jump()
    --- end`. Calling it every frame makes the character fly.
    function Jump(self): ()
end

declare extern type Camera extends PVInstance with
    --- The position of the camera and the direction it looks in. The camera looks along the
    --- `LookVector` of this value, which is -Z. Set inside a render step (a function bound with
    --- `RunService:BindToRenderStep`, a `PreRender` handler, or an action whose context has `Rate =
    --- Render`), the value is drawn exactly as set in the current frame, and it becomes the value
    --- simulation code reads when the next tick starts. Set anywhere else, it takes effect at once and
    --- is drawn interpolated between ticks. Read inside a render step, it returns the value that is
    --- being drawn. Read anywhere else, it returns the value simulation code sees.
    CFrame: CFrame
    --- The size of the area the world is drawn into, in pixels: the window in a game, or the Viewport
    --- panel in the editor. It changes when the window is resized or a phone is rotated. Use it to turn
    --- a screen position into a fraction, or to tell a tall screen from a wide one.
    read ViewportSize: Vector2
    --- The vertical field of view, in degrees. It is clamped to more than 0 and less than 180.
    FieldOfView: number
    --- The distance to the near clip plane, in meters. Geometry closer than this is not drawn. A very
    --- small value reduces depth precision across the whole view, so prefer raising it to lowering it.
    --- It applies only to a perspective camera.
    NearPlane: number
    --- The distance to the far clip plane, in meters. Geometry beyond it is not drawn.
    FarPlane: number
    --- Whether the camera is `Perspective`, for a 3D view, or `Orthographic`, for a 2D view. An
    --- orthographic camera ignores `FieldOfView` and shows `OrthographicSize` meters above and below
    --- the center of the view, at any distance.
    Projection: EnumCameraProjection
    --- Half the height of what an orthographic camera shows, in meters. The width follows from the
    --- shape of the window. Change it to zoom a 2D view. An orthographic camera draws everything from
    --- `FarPlane` behind it to `FarPlane` in front of it. Nothing shrinks with distance, so a top-down
    --- camera that stands lower than the top of a tall object still shows all of the object.
    OrthographicSize: number
    --- A plane that hides everything behind it from this camera, used while `ClipPlaneEnabled` is on.
    --- The plane passes through the position of this `CFrame`; what is in front of its `LookVector` is
    --- drawn, and what is behind is not. A mirror's camera stands behind the glass and uses the plane
    --- of the glass, so it draws the room and not the wall the mirror hangs on. `@engine/views` sets it
    --- for you. The camera must be behind the plane; if it is in front, the plane has no effect.
    ClipPlane: CFrame
    --- Whether `ClipPlane` is applied.
    ClipPlaneEnabled: boolean
    --- Returns where this camera is drawn in the current frame, which is between its positions at the
    --- last two simulation ticks. Use it for a camera that follows this one. It differs from `CFrame`
    --- only inside a render step: a function bound with `RunService:BindToRenderStep`, a `PreRender`
    --- handler, or an action whose context has `Rate = Render`. Everywhere else, including on a server,
    --- it returns `CFrame`.
    function GetRenderCFrame(self): CFrame
    --- Returns where a point in the world appears on screen: its position in pixels, its depth in
    --- meters in front of the camera, and whether it is on screen. The position is measured from the
    --- top left of the area the world is drawn into, in the same units as `UIObject.AbsolutePosition`
    --- and `InputService:GetPointerPosition`. It is valid beyond the edges of the screen, so an arrow
    --- at the border can point toward an off-screen target. With a depth of 0 or less, the point is
    --- beside or behind a perspective camera and the position is meaningless. It works for both
    --- projections. A `ScreenGui` that places elements by this position should have `ScreenInsets` off,
    --- or they are offset by the screen's cutout.
    function WorldToViewportPoint(self, position: vector): (Vector2, number, boolean)
    --- Returns the ray that a pixel looks along, as an origin and a direction of length 1. Pass it to
    --- `Workspace:Raycast` to find what is under the pointer. For a perspective camera the ray starts
    --- at the camera and passes through the pixel; for an orthographic camera it starts at the pixel's
    --- own position and points straight ahead.
    function ViewportPointToRay(self, point: Vector2): (vector, vector)
    --- Returns the position on the 2D plane that a pixel points at. Use it in a 2D game to compare a
    --- tap or a click with `Part2D.Position`. Returns nil when the camera looks along the plane or away
    --- from it.
    function ViewportPointToWorld2D(self, point: Vector2): Vector2?
end

declare extern type PointLight extends Instance with
    --- The position of the light. When the light is parented to a `BasePart` or an `Attachment`, this
    --- is relative to that parent, and the identity puts the light at the parent. Otherwise it is in
    --- world space.
    CFrame: CFrame
    --- The color of the light, multiplied by `Brightness`. It is not clamped, so a channel above 1
    --- gives an over-bright tint.
    Color: Color3
    --- How much light is emitted. At 0 the light emits nothing.
    Brightness: number
    --- The radius, in meters, beyond which the light has no effect. A smaller range is cheaper to draw.
    Range: number
    --- Whether the light shines. A disabled light does not count toward the limit on lights drawn at
    --- once; a light with a `Brightness` of 0 does.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- Whether the light casts shadows. A point light's shadow uses six of the sixteen tiles in the
    --- shadow atlas, one for each face of a cube around the light. A `SpotLight` uses one. Two point
    --- lights and four spot lights fill the atlas. When more lights ask for shadows than there are
    --- tiles, the lights that are nearest and largest on screen cast them and the others do not. The
    --- choice is stable from frame to frame, so shadows do not flicker.
    Shadows: boolean
end

declare extern type ReflectionProbe extends Instance with
    --- Whether the probe applies. A disabled probe does not count toward the number of probes a machine
    --- keeps, and surfaces in its box use the sky.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- The position the probe captures from, relative to the center of the box, in the box's own space
    --- and in meters. Move it when the center of the room is inside an obstacle such as a pillar.
    Offset: vector
    --- The distance, in meters, over which the probe fades out beyond the faces of its box, where no
    --- other probe applies. Inside the box it applies fully. Two probes whose boxes meet blend across
    --- the boundary, so a doorway between two rooms shows no seam.
    Blend: number
    --- A multiplier on the light the probe provides.
    Intensity: number
    --- Which probe applies where two boxes overlap: the one with the higher number. An integer. Give a
    --- small probe around a mirror a higher priority than the probe of the room it is in.
    Priority: number
    --- Whether reflections are corrected for the walls of the box, so that a floor reflects a wall
    --- where the wall actually is. Turn it off for a probe outdoors, whose box is not a room.
    BoxProjection: boolean
    --- Whether the probe is captured again when the lighting changes, such as when the sun moves or a
    --- light inside its box is switched on. It captures one image per frame. When it is off, the probe
    --- is captured once and again on `Refresh`.
    Dynamic: boolean
    --- Fires when the probe's capture finishes on this machine, each time `IsReady` changes from false
    --- to true. Each machine fires it at its own time.
    read Completed: Signal<()>
    --- Requests a new capture. Call it after the room changes: a wall falls, a door opens, a room is
    --- built. A probe captured while another is already in place sees that probe's light, so calling it
    --- twice adds a second bounce of light.
    function Refresh(self): ()
    --- Returns whether the probe's most recently requested capture is finished. It is false from
    --- `Refresh` until the new capture is done. It is true at once on a machine that keeps no probes,
    --- and for a probe beyond the number the machine keeps. To wait: `if not probe:IsReady() then
    --- probe.Completed:Wait() end`.
    function IsReady(self): boolean
end

declare extern type IrradianceVolume extends Instance with
    --- Whether the volume applies.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- The distance between the volume's points, in meters, along each side of the box. The minimum is
    --- 0.5. A box that needs more than 4,096 points, or more than 32 along a side, is computed as up to
    --- sixteen tiles that blend where they meet; the eight nearest the camera are drawn. A box that
    --- would need more tiles than that is given a coarser spacing, and a message is logged once.
    Spacing: number
    --- The distance, in meters, over which the volume fades out beyond the faces of its box, where no
    --- other volume applies.
    Blend: number
    --- A multiplier on the volume's light. At 1, the default, the room is as bright as its lights,
    --- openings and surfaces make it. Other values are an artistic adjustment.
    Intensity: number
    --- A color the volume's light is multiplied by. It can change every frame at no cost; nothing is
    --- computed again.
    Tint: Color3
    --- What light the volume provides. `Full` provides the light of the sky and all bounced light,
    --- replacing the sky's light and `Lighting.Ambient`. `BounceOnly` provides only bounced light,
    --- added to them. Changing it computes the volume again.
    Mode: EnumIrradianceMode
    --- How many times light bounces, from 1 to 3. Each bounce costs as much to compute as the first.
    Bounces: number
    --- A file that holds this volume's computed light, as an `asset://` path ending in `.irradiance`.
    --- Write it with the editor's Bake command on the volume, or with `engine-host <project>
    --- --bake-irradiance`. A machine that computes volumes loads the file instead of casting rays, so
    --- the room is lit from the first frame. A machine that computes none (`GlobalIllumination` below
    --- `High`, such as a phone) reads one color from the file, the average of the whole volume, and
    --- lights the box with it in place of the flat ambient light. That gives the room its overall tone
    --- at no cost per frame. The file is valid only for the volume it was baked for. If the box,
    --- `Spacing`, `Bounces` or `Mode` differ, the file is rejected with one logged message and the
    --- volume is computed as if it had no file. With `Dynamic` on, the file provides the first light
    --- and the volume is computed again as the lighting changes. `Refresh` computes it by rays. The
    --- file is not used with `FollowsCamera`. Bake again after changing the room. The editor and
    --- development runs log a message once when the objects in the box differ from those it was baked
    --- with.
    Baked: Content
    --- Whether the volume moves with the camera, to provide bounced light outdoors, where no box can
    --- enclose the area. The part then gives only the volume's size; its position is ignored. The
    --- volume is centered on the camera on a grid of its own cells and follows it a few cells at a
    --- time. Points that are in both the old and the new position keep their light, so a move costs
    --- only the new points. A room's own volume still applies inside the room; this one lights what no
    --- other volume covers.
    FollowsCamera: boolean
    --- With `FollowsCamera`: the number of nested levels, from 1 to 3. Each level after the first is
    --- twice as large around the same center, with points twice as far apart, so light is detailed near
    --- the camera and coarse far from it. A part of 40 by 16 by 40 at a `Spacing` of 2 with three
    --- levels lights 80 meters in every direction.
    Levels: number
    --- Whether the volume is computed again when the lighting changes, such as when the sun moves or a
    --- light that reaches its box is switched on. The work is spread over several frames, and the
    --- picture changes from the old light to the new once the new is complete. When it is off, the
    --- volume is computed once and again on `Refresh`.
    Dynamic: boolean
    --- Fires when the volume finishes computing on this machine, each time `IsReady` changes from false
    --- to true. Each machine fires it at its own time, so game rules must not depend on it.
    read Completed: Signal<()>
    --- Requests that the volume be computed again. Call it after the room changes: a wall falls or a
    --- room is built.
    function Refresh(self): ()
    --- Returns how much of the computation is done, from 0 to 1. Use it for a loading screen. It is 1
    --- when the volume's own light is being drawn, and also on a machine that computes none. It stays 1
    --- while a `Dynamic` volume is being computed again after the lighting changed.
    function GetProgress(self): number
    --- Returns whether the volume's own light is being drawn, for the most recent request. It is false
    --- from `Refresh`, or from a change to the box, `Spacing`, `Bounces` or `Mode`, until the volume is
    --- computed again. It is true at once on a machine that computes none. To wait: `if not
    --- volume:IsReady() then volume.Completed:Wait() end`.
    function IsReady(self): boolean
end

declare extern type SequencePlayer extends Instance with
    --- The sequence file, as an `asset://` path ending in `.sequence.json`. It is read when first
    --- played and again whenever the file changes.
    Sequence: Content
    --- Whether the sequence is advancing. Change it with `Play` and `Pause`.
    read Playing: boolean
    --- The current position in the sequence, in seconds. Setting it seeks: every track is put in the
    --- state it has at that time, and no event fires. The sequence stays playing or paused as it was. A
    --- value beyond `Length` is clamped to `Length`.
    TimePosition: number
    --- How fast the sequence's time runs relative to simulation time. Lines and sounds always play at
    --- their own speed.
    Speed: number
    --- Whether the sequence starts again when it reaches the end. A looped sequence does not fire
    --- `Finished` on its own; `Skip` still ends it.
    Looped: boolean
    --- Whether each machine's main view is drawn through the camera track while the sequence plays.
    --- When it is off, the camera track is ignored.
    TakesCamera: boolean
    --- What happens to player input while the sequence plays. `Suspend` holds the local players'
    --- movement and actions at rest on this machine and restores them when the sequence ends. Input
    --- events still reach scripts, so a game can bind a key to `Skip`.
    Controls: EnumSequenceControl
    --- The length of the sequence, in seconds. It is available as soon as `Sequence` is set, and is 0
    --- if the file does not exist. The length is the end of the last key or the last line, or the
    --- length the file states if that is greater. A line lasts as long as its longest language, so the
    --- length is the same in every language.
    read Length: number
    --- The name of the hold the sequence is waiting at, or an empty string. A key on a `hold` track
    --- stops the sequence's time until `Release` is called, to wait for a choice or a button press.
    --- What the sequence already started continues, such as a playing clip, and nothing new starts.
    read Held: string
    --- Fires when the sequence ends: it reached its end, or `Skip` was called, in which case `skipped`
    --- is true. `Stop` does not fire it.
    read Finished: Signal<boolean>
    --- Fires when the sequence reaches a key on a `hold` track and starts waiting for `Release`. Passes
    --- the hold's name. It fires on every machine.
    read HoldReached: Signal<string>
    --- Fires when the sequence reaches a marker, either by playing through it or by `SkipToNext`
    --- landing on it. Passes the marker's name.
    read MarkerReached: Signal<string>
    --- Fires when the sequence reaches a key on an event track. Passes the event's `name` and its
    --- `data` as written in the file: a number, a boolean, a string, a list of numbers, or nil.
    --- `skipped` is true for an event fired by `Skip` on the way to the end. A handler that changes the
    --- game, such as opening a door, should still act; one that only plays an effect can ignore it.
    --- Seeking fires no events.
    read EventReached: Signal<string, any, boolean>
    --- Plays the sequence from its current position: from the start the first time or after it ended,
    --- and otherwise from where `Pause` left it.
    function Play(self): ()
    --- Stops the sequence's time at its current position. The camera stays on the sequence, lines that
    --- are being spoken finish, and `Play` resumes it.
    function Pause(self): ()
    --- Ends the sequence and leaves the world as it is at that moment. Properties keep the values the
    --- sequence gave them, clips fade out, the lines and sounds it started end, characters it was
    --- carrying are set down, the camera is returned, and its time goes back to 0. No event fires,
    --- including `Finished`.
    function Stop(self): ()
    --- Ends the sequence and leaves the world as the sequence would have left it at its end. Every
    --- keyed property and morph target takes the value of its last key, and every event between the
    --- current position and the end fires in order with `skipped` true. The lines and sounds it started
    --- end, clips fade out, characters are set down where their last keys put them, and the camera is
    --- returned. Then `Finished` fires with `skipped` true.
    function Skip(self): ()
    --- Continues from the hold the sequence is waiting at. Does nothing if it is not held. `Play` does
    --- the same. In a multiplayer match only the server can call it, like `Play`: a hold is held and
    --- released on every machine.
    function Release(self): ()
    --- Returns the sequence's markers, in order of time: the keys of its `marker` track, which usually
    --- mark where each shot begins. Returns an empty list if the file has none or does not exist.
    function GetMarkers(self): { { Name: string, Time: number } }
    --- Seeks to a marker by name and plays from there. The seek works like setting `TimePosition`:
    --- every track is put in the state it has at that time, and no event fires. Returns false and does
    --- nothing if the file has no marker of that name.
    function PlayFrom(self, marker: string): boolean
    --- Skips to the next marker after the current position and plays on from there. The world is left
    --- as the sequence would have left it at that time: keyed properties take the values their keys
    --- give them, events in between fire with `skipped` true, and lines and sounds in between are not
    --- played. Holds on the way are passed. Returns false and does nothing if there is no later marker;
    --- use `Skip` to end the sequence.
    function SkipToNext(self): boolean
    --- Loads a sequence from a table instead of a file. The table has the same shape as a
    --- `.sequence.json` file: `{ format = "sequence", version = 1, bindings = {...}, tracks = {...} }`.
    --- `Sequence` is set to a generated name, and `Play` plays the table. Use it for a scene that the
    --- game builds as it runs. Raises an error that names the place if the table is not a valid
    --- sequence. The sequence exists only on the machine that loads it. Loaded on a server, it changes
    --- the world and every client sees the change, but the visual tracks (camera, fade, bars, morph
    --- targets) are not drawn on clients, which do not have the table. For a scene that every machine
    --- should see in full, use a file, write the parts that vary as `"$Name"` and set them as
    --- attributes.
    function LoadSequence(self, sequence: { [string]: any }): ()
    --- Binds a function that can adjust the camera on every frame in which this sequence has the view.
    --- Use it for what a file cannot know, such as a wall the scene happens to be next to. The function
    --- receives the `CFrame` the sequence computed, the field of view in degrees and the sequence's
    --- time in seconds. It returns a `CFrame` to draw instead, then optionally a field of view; return
    --- nil for either to keep the sequence's value. It runs in a render step, so `GetRenderCFrame`
    --- returns drawn positions inside it, and it must not yield. A player has one such function;
    --- binding again replaces it. It applies only on the machine that binds it.
    function BindCamera(self, fn: (cframe: CFrame, fieldOfView: number, time: number) -> (CFrame?, ...number)): ()
    --- Removes the function bound with `BindCamera`. Does nothing if none is bound.
    function UnbindCamera(self): ()
end

declare extern type SequenceBinding extends Instance with
    --- The instance the name refers to. A track targets this instance, or a descendant given by a path
    --- from it, such as `Lamp/PointLight`.
    Target: Instance?
end

declare extern type Decal extends Instance with
    --- The position and orientation of the box, relative to the parent part if there is one. The image
    --- lies along the box's right and up axes and is projected along its look direction.
    CFrame: CFrame
    --- The width, height and depth of the box, in meters. Only surfaces inside the box receive the
    --- image, so a shallow box marks one wall and a deep one wraps around a corner.
    Size: vector
    --- The image to project. Its alpha channel sets how much of it is applied. With no texture, the
    --- decal applies `Color` alone.
    Texture: Content
    --- A color the image is multiplied by.
    Color: Color3
    --- How transparent the decal is, from 0 (the image as it is) to 1 (invisible).
    Transparency: number
    --- How the image is combined with the surface: multiplied into it, laid over it, or added to it.
    BlendMode: EnumDecalBlendMode
    --- How brightly the image glows on its own, in addition to the light that falls on it. 0 is no
    --- glow; a value above 1 is bright enough to bloom. It applies to the `Alpha` and `Additive` blend
    --- modes only.
    Emissive: number
end

declare extern type CameraTexture extends Instance with
    --- The camera to draw from. With nil, nothing is drawn and the texture keeps its last picture.
    Camera: Camera?
    --- The name of the texture: it is available as `view://` followed by this name. If two have the
    --- same name, the one created first draws and a message is logged for the other. The texture is
    --- black until the first picture is drawn.
    ViewName: string
    --- The size of the texture, in pixels. Each side is limited to `[render] max_view_resolution` (1024
    --- by default); a larger size is scaled down with its aspect ratio kept.
    Resolution: Vector2
    --- Whether the view is drawn. A disabled view keeps its last picture and costs nothing.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- The number of frames between pictures: at 1 the view is drawn every frame, and at 4 every fourth
    --- frame, for a quarter of the cost.
    UpdateInterval: number
    --- How the view is drawn. `Simple` draws without shadows or post-processing effects, which suits a
    --- monitor feed. `Full` draws the way the main view does.
    Quality: EnumViewQuality
end

declare extern type SubWorld extends Instance with
    --- The scene file to run, relative to the content folder, such as `scenes/arcade.scene.json`. It is
    --- read when `Load` starts the scene; a later change takes effect at the next `Load`.
    Scene: string
    --- The name of the texture the sub-world is drawn into: it is available as `view://` followed by
    --- this name. With an empty name nothing is drawn, and the world still runs.
    ViewName: string
    --- The size of the texture, in pixels, with the same limit as `CameraTexture.Resolution`.
    Resolution: Vector2
    --- The number of frames between pictures: at 1 the view is drawn every frame. The world ticks every
    --- tick whatever this value is.
    UpdateInterval: number
    --- How the view is drawn. `Simple` draws without shadows or post-processing effects. `Full` draws
    --- the way the main view does.
    Quality: EnumViewQuality
    --- Whether the sub-world ticks. When it is off, the world is paused and its picture stays as it
    --- was.
    Running: boolean
    --- Fires when the sub-world's world is running and its scripts have started.
    read Loaded: Signal<()>
    --- Fires with the values the sub-world passed to `SceneService:SendToHost`, one tick after it sent
    --- them.
    read Received: Signal<...any>
    --- Starts the scene. It returns at once: the world starts after the current tick, and `Loaded`
    --- fires when its scripts have started. Does nothing if the sub-world is already loaded. Raises an
    --- error if `[render] max_sub_worlds` are already running, or if it is called inside a sub-world.
    function Load(self): ()
    --- Stops the sub-world and discards its world, after the current tick. Its picture stays until the
    --- next `Load` draws over it. Destroying the `SubWorld` does the same.
    function Unload(self): ()
    --- Returns whether the sub-world's world exists, running or paused. It is true from `Loaded` until
    --- `Unload`.
    function IsLoaded(self): boolean
    --- Holds the sub-world's `InputAction` named `action` at `value` from its next tick until it is set
    --- again. `value` is true or false for a `Bool` action, or a number, a `Vector2` or a `vector` for
    --- a direction. No other input reaches the sub-world: the keyboard, mouse and gamepads belong to
    --- this world, which decides what to pass on.
    function SetInputState(self, action: string, value: boolean | number | Vector2 | vector): ()
    --- Sends values into the sub-world, where `SceneService.HostMessageReceived` fires with them on its
    --- next tick. It accepts the values a `RemoteEvent` accepts, except instances, because the two
    --- worlds share none. The values are dropped if the sub-world is not loaded.
    function Send(self, ...: any): ()
end

declare extern type ParticleEmitter extends Instance with
    --- Whether the emitter emits continuously. `Emit` works whether it is on or off.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- The number of particles emitted per second while the emitter is enabled.
    Rate: number
    --- How long each particle lives, in seconds.
    Lifetime: number
    --- The speed of a particle when it is emitted, in meters per second, along the emitter's up
    --- direction.
    Speed: number
    --- The largest angle, in degrees, between a particle's direction and the emitter's up direction. 0
    --- is a jet and 180 is a sphere.
    SpreadAngle: number
    --- A constant acceleration applied to every particle, in meters per second squared, in world space.
    --- `vector.create(0, -9.81, 0)` is gravity; a small upward value suits rising heat.
    Acceleration: vector
    --- The fraction of its velocity a particle loses each second. Use it for smoke, which slows; leave
    --- it at 0 for sparks.
    Drag: number
    --- Whether the workspace's wind carries the particles. The wind at each particle's position is
    --- added to its motion. Off by default.
    WindAffectsDrift: boolean
    --- The color of a particle when it is emitted.
    Color: Color3
    --- The color of a particle at the end of its life. The color changes gradually from `Color` to
    --- this.
    ColorEnd: Color3
    --- The width of a particle when it is emitted, in meters.
    Size: number
    --- The width of a particle at the end of its life, in meters.
    SizeEnd: number
    --- How transparent a particle is when it is emitted, from 0 (opaque) to 1 (invisible).
    Transparency: number
    --- How transparent a particle is at the end of its life, from 0 (opaque) to 1 (invisible). With the
    --- default, each particle fades out as it dies.
    TransparencyEnd: number
    --- How much a particle adds its light to what is behind it instead of covering it, from 0 to 1. At
    --- 0 it is blended over the background. At 1 it is added to it and never darkens anything, which
    --- suits fire, sparks and magic.
    LightEmission: number
    --- A multiplier on the particle's color. It is not clamped; a value above 1 makes a particle bright
    --- enough to bloom.
    Brightness: number
    --- The shape a particle is drawn as when the emitter has no `Texture`.
    Shape: EnumParticleShape
    --- The image each particle is drawn as, facing the camera. Its color is multiplied by the
    --- particle's color, and its alpha channel gives the particle's outline. With no texture, `Shape`
    --- is drawn. With `FlipbookColumns` or `FlipbookRows` above 1, the image is a grid of animation
    --- frames.
    Texture: Content
    --- The number of frames across `Texture`, from 1 to 16. Frames are read left to right, then top to
    --- bottom.
    FlipbookColumns: number
    --- The number of rows of frames in `Texture`, from 1 to 16.
    FlipbookRows: number
    --- The speed of the flipbook animation, in frames per second, when `FlipbookMode` is `Loop`.
    FlipbookFramerate: number
    --- How a particle plays the frames of the flipbook: in a loop, once over its life, or as one still
    --- frame chosen for that particle.
    FlipbookMode: EnumParticleFlipbookMode
    --- The angle, in degrees, a particle is rotated by when it is emitted, in the plane facing the
    --- camera.
    Rotation: number
    --- The largest random angle, in degrees, added to or subtracted from `Rotation` for each particle.
    --- At 180, particles are emitted at any angle.
    RotationSpread: number
    --- How fast a particle rotates, in degrees per second. A negative value rotates the other way.
    RotationSpeed: number
    --- The largest random amount, in degrees per second, added to or subtracted from `RotationSpeed`
    --- for each particle.
    RotationSpeedSpread: number
    --- A color over a particle's life, from 0 when it is emitted to 1 when it dies. It is multiplied by
    --- the color that `Color` and `ColorEnd` give. White changes nothing.
    ColorOverLife: ColorSequence
    --- A scale over a particle's life, multiplied by the size that `Size` and `SizeEnd` give. For a
    --- puff that swells and shrinks, use a curve that rises and falls. A value of 1 changes nothing.
    SizeOverLife: NumberSequence
    --- A transparency over a particle's life, combined with the one that `Transparency` and
    --- `TransparencyEnd` give, like one transparent layer over another. For a fade in and out, use a
    --- curve from 1 down to 0 and back to 1. A value of 0 changes nothing.
    TransparencyOverLife: NumberSequence
    --- What the particles collide with: nothing, the surfaces on screen, the terrain, or both.
    Collision: EnumParticleCollision
    --- What a particle does when it hits something: bounce, stick or die.
    CollisionResponse: EnumParticleCollisionResponse
    --- The fraction of its speed into a surface that a particle keeps when it bounces, from 0 (none) to
    --- 1 (all). A particle that bounces too slowly to be seen comes to rest.
    Bounce: number
    --- The fraction of its speed along a surface that a particle loses each time it touches, from 0 (it
    --- slides forever) to 1 (it stops where it lands).
    Friction: number
    --- The distance from a particle's center at which it collides, in meters. At 0, half the particle's
    --- size is used.
    CollisionRadius: number
    --- Whether the particles are simulated on the CPU or on the GPU. The GPU handles a hundred thousand
    --- particles where the CPU handles a few thousand. A machine without compute shaders always
    --- simulates on the CPU, and logs a message once.
    Simulation: EnumParticleSimulation
    --- Emits `count` particles at once, whether or not the emitter is enabled. Use it for a burst such
    --- as an explosion. One call emits at most 1,000.
    function Emit(self, count: number): ()
end

declare extern type Highlight extends Instance with
    --- The part or model to highlight. A model highlights every part in it. With nil, the highlight's
    --- parent is highlighted.
    Adornee: (BasePart | Model)?
    --- The color of the tint drawn over the whole shape.
    FillColor: Color3
    --- How transparent the fill is, from 0 (it hides what it covers) to 1 (only the outline shows).
    FillTransparency: number
    --- The color of the line around the edge of the shape.
    OutlineColor: Color3
    --- How transparent the outline is, from 0 (solid) to 1 (no outline).
    OutlineTransparency: number
    --- Whether the highlight shows through objects in front of the shape, or only where the shape
    --- itself is visible.
    DepthMode: EnumHighlightDepthMode
    --- Whether the highlight is drawn.
    Enabled: boolean
end

declare extern type Beam extends Instance with
    --- The attachment where the beam starts.
    Attachment0: Attachment?
    --- The attachment where the beam ends.
    Attachment1: Attachment?
    --- The color of the beam along its length, from `Attachment0` at 0 to `Attachment1` at 1. It is
    --- multiplied by the texture.
    Color: ColorSequence
    --- How transparent the beam is along its length, from 0 (opaque) to 1 (invisible).
    Transparency: NumberSequence
    --- The width of the beam at `Attachment0`, in meters.
    Width0: number
    --- The width of the beam at `Attachment1`, in meters.
    Width1: number
    --- How far the beam travels from `Attachment0` along that attachment's X axis before it bends
    --- toward the other end, in meters. With 0 at both ends the beam is straight. A negative value
    --- leaves in the opposite direction.
    CurveSize0: number
    --- How far before `Attachment1` the beam is already traveling along that attachment's X axis, in
    --- meters.
    CurveSize1: number
    --- The number of straight pieces a curved beam is drawn with, from 1 to 64. A straight beam needs
    --- only 1.
    Segments: number
    --- The image drawn along the beam, with U along its length and V across it. With no texture, the
    --- beam is a plain band of `Color`.
    Texture: Content
    --- The length of one repeat of the texture, in meters, in the `Wrap` and `Static` texture modes.
    TextureLength: number
    --- How the texture is laid along the beam.
    TextureMode: EnumTextureMode
    --- How fast the texture scrolls from `Attachment0` toward `Attachment1`: in repeats per second in
    --- `Stretch` mode, and in meters per second in `Wrap` mode. In `Static` mode it does not scroll.
    TextureSpeed: number
    --- Whether the band turns about its own length to face the camera. When it is off, the band lies in
    --- the plane of its attachments' Y axes, as a flat ribbon with two sides.
    FaceCamera: boolean
    --- How much the beam adds its color to what is behind it instead of covering it, from 0 (an
    --- ordinary surface) to 1 (pure light).
    LightEmission: number
    --- How much the scene's lighting affects the beam's color, from 0 (always its own color) to 1 (lit
    --- like a surface).
    LightInfluence: number
    --- The distance, in meters, the beam is drawn toward the camera. Use it so a beam that lies on a
    --- wall is not cut off by the wall.
    ZOffset: number
    --- Whether the beam is drawn.
    Enabled: boolean
end

declare extern type Trail extends Instance with
    --- The attachment at one edge of the ribbon.
    Attachment0: Attachment?
    --- The attachment at the other edge of the ribbon.
    Attachment1: Attachment?
    --- How long a piece of the trail lasts, in seconds, from the moment the attachments were there.
    Lifetime: number
    --- The distance the attachments must move before the trail gains a new piece, in meters. A smaller
    --- value is smoother and costs more.
    MinLength: number
    --- The greatest length of the trail, in meters. Older pieces beyond it are removed. At 0, only
    --- `Lifetime` limits the trail.
    MaxLength: number
    --- The color of a piece over its life, from 0 when it is new to 1 at `Lifetime`. It is multiplied
    --- by the texture.
    Color: ColorSequence
    --- How transparent a piece is over its life, from 0 (opaque) to 1 (invisible).
    Transparency: NumberSequence
    --- The width of a piece over its life, as a fraction of the distance between the attachments.
    WidthScale: NumberSequence
    --- The image drawn along the trail, with U along its length and V across it. With no texture, the
    --- trail is a plain band of `Color`.
    Texture: Content
    --- The length of one repeat of the texture, in meters, in the `Wrap` and `Static` texture modes.
    TextureLength: number
    --- How the texture is laid along the trail.
    TextureMode: EnumTextureMode
    --- Whether the ribbon turns about its center line to face the camera, as wide as the distance
    --- between the attachments. When it is off, the ribbon is the surface the two attachments swept.
    FaceCamera: boolean
    --- How much the trail adds its color to what is behind it instead of covering it, from 0 (an
    --- ordinary surface) to 1 (pure light).
    LightEmission: number
    --- How much the scene's lighting affects the trail's color, from 0 (always its own color) to 1 (lit
    --- like a surface).
    LightInfluence: number
    --- Whether the trail grows. When it is off, the existing trail fades out over its `Lifetime`.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- Removes the whole trail at once. Call it when the object teleports, so the ribbon does not
    --- stretch from the old position to the new one.
    function Clear(self): ()
end

declare extern type SpotLight extends Instance with
    --- The position and direction of the light. When the light is parented to a `BasePart` or an
    --- `Attachment`, this is relative to that parent, and the identity puts the light at the parent.
    --- Otherwise it is in world space.
    CFrame: CFrame
    --- The color of the light, multiplied by `Brightness`.
    Color: Color3
    --- How much light is emitted. At 0 the light emits nothing.
    Brightness: number
    --- The distance, in meters, beyond which the light has no effect.
    Range: number
    --- The full width of the cone, in degrees.
    Angle: number
    --- Whether the light shines. A disabled light does not count toward the limit on lights drawn at
    --- once; a light with a `Brightness` of 0 does.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- Whether the light casts shadows. A spot light's shadow uses one of the sixteen tiles in the
    --- shadow atlas. A `PointLight` uses six, and both kinds share the same atlas. When more lights ask
    --- for shadows than there are tiles, the lights that are nearest and largest on screen cast them
    --- and the others do not. The choice is stable from frame to frame, so shadows do not flicker.
    Shadows: boolean
end

declare extern type PostEffect extends Instance with
    --- Whether the effect is applied. A disabled effect is not the same as no effect: a disabled
    --- `BloomEffect` still controls bloom, and turns it off.
    Enabled: boolean
end

declare extern type BloomEffect extends PostEffect with
    --- The strength of the glow, as a multiple of the engine's default. 1 is the default look, 2 is
    --- twice the glow, and 0 is none.
    Intensity: number
    --- How far the glow spreads, from 0 to 56. 24 is the engine's default. 48 spreads the same light
    --- about twice as far, and fainter.
    Size: number
    --- How bright a pixel must be, after exposure, before it glows. Exposure brings the average of a
    --- frame to about 0.45, so the default blooms pixels a little more than twice as bright as the
    --- average. At 0, everything glows a little.
    Threshold: number
end

declare extern type ColorCorrectionEffect extends PostEffect with
    --- An amount of light added to every pixel, from -1 to 1. The average of a frame is about 0.45
    --- after exposure, so 0.1 is a gentle lift and -0.2 is clearly darker. The darkest pixels stop at
    --- black.
    Brightness: number
    --- How far every pixel is pushed away from the frame's average brightness, from -1 to 1. At 0
    --- nothing changes, at 0.5 the difference grows by half, and at -1 the picture is flattened to a
    --- single gray.
    Contrast: number
    --- How vivid colors are, from -1 to 1. -1 is black and white, 0 changes nothing, and 1 doubles the
    --- distance of each color from gray.
    Saturation: number
    --- A color the picture is multiplied by. White changes nothing; a warm white warms the picture.
    TintColor: Color3
end

declare extern type BlurEffect extends PostEffect with
    --- How far the light of a pixel is spread, in pixels of a picture 1,080 lines tall. It scales with
    --- the window, so a blur looks the same at any resolution. 0 is no blur and costs nothing. 100 is
    --- the maximum and leaves no detail.
    Size: number
end

declare extern type DepthOfFieldEffect extends PostEffect with
    --- The distance from the camera that is sharpest, in meters.
    FocusDistance: number
    --- The distance on either side of `FocusDistance` that stays fully sharp, in meters. The blur
    --- begins beyond it.
    InFocusRadius: number
    --- How much what is nearer than the sharp band is blurred, from 0 (not at all) to 1.
    NearIntensity: number
    --- How much what is farther than the sharp band is blurred, from 0 (not at all) to 1.
    FarIntensity: number
end

declare extern type SunRaysEffect extends PostEffect with
    --- The brightness of the shafts, from 0 to 1.
    Intensity: number
    --- How far the shafts extend from the sun, from 0 (a halo) to 1 (across the screen).
    Spread: number
end

declare extern type Atmosphere extends Instance with
    --- How thick the air is, from 0 to 1. At 0 the air is perfectly clear. At the default, half of an
    --- object 280 meters away still shows through, at the height of `Offset`. At 1, half is hidden
    --- within 35 meters.
    Density: number
    --- The height, in meters, at which the air has exactly its `Density`. The air is thicker below this
    --- height and thinner above it, at the rate `Decay` sets. Raising it lifts the haze up the sides of
    --- a valley.
    Offset: number
    --- The color of the air when it is lit. Distant objects fade toward it, and the sky turns toward it
    --- at the horizon. Its brightness still depends on the height of the sun, so the same color is dark
    --- at night.
    Color: Color3
    --- How quickly the air thins with height, from 0 to 1. At 0 it is equally thick at every height. At
    --- the default it halves every 100 meters of height, and at 1 every 10 meters.
    Decay: number
    --- The strength of the bright area the air forms around the sun, from 0 to 10. It brightens the sky
    --- near the sun and distant objects seen in its direction.
    Glare: number
    --- Extra thickness near the horizon, from 0 to 10. It blends the sky's horizon into the color of
    --- the air and hides far distances more than `Density` alone.
    Haze: number
end

declare extern type Sky extends Instance with
    --- The image toward +Z, before `SkyboxOrientation` rotates the set. If none of the six images is
    --- set, the engine draws its own sky from the time of day.
