    --- The image directly below.
    SkyboxDown: Content
    --- The image toward -Z, the direction a camera with no rotation looks in.
    SkyboxFront: Content
    --- The image toward -X.
    SkyboxLeft: Content
    --- The image toward +X.
    SkyboxRight: Content
    --- The image directly above.
    SkyboxUp: Content
    --- A rotation applied to the six images together, in degrees about X, Y and Z. Use it to move
    --- something painted into the images, such as a mountain range, to where the world needs it.
    SkyboxOrientation: vector
    --- An image drawn as the sun's disc, lit by the sun's color. With none, a round disc with a soft
    --- edge is drawn.
    SunTexture: Content
    --- An image drawn as the moon's disc at night. With none, a plain pale disc is drawn.
    MoonTexture: Content
    --- The apparent width of the sun, in degrees, greater than 0 and at most 60. The real sun is about
    --- half a degree wide; the default is larger so that a sunset is visible in a short game day.
    SunAngularSize: number
    --- The apparent width of the moon, in degrees, greater than 0 and at most 60.
    MoonAngularSize: number
    --- The number of stars over the whole sky at night, from 0 to 10,000. They appear as the sun sets,
    --- and they are the same stars in the same places every night.
    StarCount: number
    --- Whether the sun, the moon and the stars are drawn. When it is off, only the images and the
    --- clouds are drawn; the light still comes from the sun's direction.
    CelestialBodiesShown: boolean
    --- How much of the sky the clouds cover, from 0 (none) to 1 (overcast). Clouds drift on simulation
    --- time, so they stop while the game is paused and move identically in a replay.
    CloudCover: number
    --- How thick each cloud is, from 0 (wisps that the sky shows through) to 1 (solid, with dark
    --- undersides).
    CloudDensity: number
    --- The color of the clouds where the sun lights them.
    CloudColor: Color3
end

declare extern type InputContext extends Instance with
    --- The local player whose character input receives this context's actions. With nil, the primary
    --- local player is used. Only a local player that is present is accepted. If the player is removed,
    --- the context's input does not move to the primary player.
    Player: Player?
    --- The id of the gamepad this context reads. At 0, the input of all gamepads is combined. With a
    --- positive id, only that gamepad is read, and a disconnected one reads as neutral. Keyboard, mouse
    --- and touch bindings are not affected. A context with `Sink` on consumes input only from the
    --- gamepad it reads.
    GamepadId: number
    --- Whether this context and its actions are active. A disabled context consumes no input, so a
    --- lower-priority context bound to the same key starts receiving it in the same tick.
    Enabled: boolean
    --- The order in which contexts receive input, highest first. It decides only which context is
    --- offered an input first; `Rate` sets when actions are dispatched. Contexts with equal priority
    --- are ordered the same way on every run.
    Priority: number
    --- Whether this context consumes the inputs its bindings use, hiding them from every lower-priority
    --- context. A modal dialog should sink; a HUD should not. Only the bound inputs are consumed: a
    --- sinking context that binds Escape hides Escape and leaves W available.
    Sink: boolean
    --- Whether the context's actions are dispatched on simulation ticks or on rendered frames.
    --- `Simulation`, the default, is deterministic and is recorded in a replay; use it for gameplay.
    --- `Render` is for camera look and UI; an action read there depends on the frame rate.
    Rate: EnumInputRate
end

declare extern type InputAction extends Instance with
    --- The kind of value the action produces, which is what `GetState` returns. Every binding of the
    --- action produces this type, whether it is a key, a stick or an on-screen button.
