declare extern type Workspace extends Instance with
    --- The acceleration applied to every unanchored part, in meters per second squared. It is signed:
    --- the default is negative and points down.
    Gravity: vector
    --- The direction and speed of the wind, in meters per second. It is visual only: it moves particles
    --- that drift with it, foliage, and surface shaders that read it, and it does not push physics
    --- bodies. Zero, the default, is no wind. `GetWindAt` returns the wind at a point, including gusts.
    GlobalWind: vector
    --- How much the wind's speed rises and falls in gusts, from 0 (steady) to 1. Gusts travel downwind
    --- at the wind's speed, so one can be seen crossing a field.
    WindGusts: number
    --- How much the wind's direction varies from place to place, from 0 (uniform) to 1.
    WindTurbulence: number
    --- How the window is divided between the local players on this machine. - `None` draws one picture
    --- through `CurrentCamera`. - `Columns`, `Rows` and `Grid` give every local player that has a
    --- `Camera` its own view, in the order the players joined: side by side, stacked, or in quarters. -
    --- `Dynamic` draws one picture while two players are close together, and splits it along a line
    --- when they move apart (see `SplitBlend` and `SplitAngle`). Each view is drawn at the resolution
    --- of its part of the window, with everything a full picture has. The views share the world, its
    --- animation and its reflections, and each has its own culling, shadows, exposure and
    --- anti-aliasing. While a sequence that takes the camera plays, the window shows one picture; the
    --- views return when it ends. The setting is local to this machine and is not replicated.
    ScreenSplit: EnumScreenSplit
    --- With `ScreenSplit` set to `Dynamic`: how far the picture is split, from 0 to 1. At 0 the window
    --- shows one picture through `CurrentCamera`. Above 0 the views of the first two local players are
    --- joined along a line through the center of the window. The line is `SplitLineThickness` thick at
    --- 1 and thinner below it. A game sets this value every frame; `@engine/splitscreen` does it for a
    --- top-down game.
    SplitBlend: number
    --- With `ScreenSplit` set to `Dynamic`: the direction on screen from the first player to the
    --- second, in degrees. 0 puts the second player to the right of the first, and 90 puts it above.
    --- The dividing line runs perpendicular to this direction, and each player's view is on that
    --- player's side.
    SplitAngle: number
    --- The thickness of the line between two views, in pixels of a window 1,080 pixels tall. 0 draws no
    --- line.
    SplitLineThickness: number
    --- The color of the line between two views.
    SplitLineColor: Color3
    --- The camera the world is drawn from. With nil, nothing is drawn.
    CurrentCamera: Camera?
    --- The world's terrain, or nil if the world has none. A `Terrain` is an ordinary instance under the
    --- workspace, and this property refers to the one the engine uses as the world's ground.
    read Terrain: Terrain?
    --- Returns the distance in meters from `position` to the nearest player's focus, or `math.huge`
    --- when no player has one. A focus is their `ReplicationFocus`, or their character, as the physics
    --- step last took them. Use it to decide how much a far thing needs to do; `@engine/npclod` calls
    --- an NPC's code by it.
    function GetNearestPlayerDistance(self, position: vector): number
    --- Returns the wind at a point at the current simulation time, in meters per second: `GlobalWind`
    --- with its gusts and turbulence. The renderer uses the same function, so a flag, a sail or a sound
    --- driven by this value matches what is drawn.
    function GetWindAt(self, position: vector): vector
    --- Returns the nearest thing that a ray from `origin` hits, or nil. The length of `direction` is
    --- the length of the ray, so `direction * 100` casts a ray 100 meters long. When two surfaces are
    --- hit at the same distance, the result is the same on every run.
    function Raycast(self, origin: vector, direction: vector, params: RaycastParams?): RaycastResult?
    --- Returns the nearest `Part2D` or `Tilemap2D` that a ray on the 2D plane hits, or nil. The length
    --- of `direction` is the length of the ray. Sensors and parts with `CanCollide` off are ignored.
    --- When two hits are at the same distance, the result is the same on every run.
    function Raycast2D(self, origin: Vector2, direction: Vector2, params: RaycastParams?): RaycastResult2D?
    --- Returns the nearest thing that a sphere moved along a ray hits, or nil. Use it instead of
    --- `Raycast` for a camera boom or a character's ground check, where a thin ray would pass through a
    --- gap that the object itself does not fit through.
    function Spherecast(self, origin: vector, radius: number, direction: vector, params: RaycastParams?): RaycastResult?
    --- Returns what a ray hits in the world as `player`'s machine was drawing it. Every other player's
    --- `Character`, and every part with `BasePart.LagCompensated`, is placed where that machine drew
    --- it, at the moment it was showing; everything else is as it is now. Check a shot with it on the
    --- server, and a player with real latency hits the moving target they aimed at. The rewind is at
    --- most `rewind_cap_ms` under `[network]` in `project.toml`, 200 by default: a slower shooter's
    --- shot is checked against the world that long ago, so a target who reached cover more than that
    --- long ago is safe. The shooter's own character is where it is now. Only the server calls it; on a
    --- client it raises.
    function RaycastAsSeenBy(self, player: Player, origin: vector, direction: vector, params: RaycastParams?): RaycastResult?
    --- `RaycastAsSeenBy` for a sphere moved along the ray: a thick projectile, or a melee swing,
    --- checked against what its player saw.
    function SpherecastAsSeenBy(self, player: Player, origin: vector, radius: number, direction: vector, params: RaycastParams?): RaycastResult?
    --- Returns every part that overlaps an oriented box, as a new array in a stable order, with one
    --- entry per part. `size` is the full extent of the box, as in `BasePart.Size`.
    function GetBodiesInBox(self, cframe: CFrame, size: vector, params: RaycastParams?): {BasePart}
    --- Returns every part that overlaps a sphere, as a new array in a stable order, with one entry per
    --- part. Use it to find what an explosion reaches or what is within arm's length.
    function GetBodiesInSphere(self, position: vector, radius: number, params: RaycastParams?): {BasePart}
end

declare extern type Lighting extends Instance with
    --- The time of day, in hours from 0 to 24. A value outside the range wraps around, so a script can
    --- add to it every tick. Together with `GeographicLatitude`, it sets the direction of the sun.
    ClockTime: number
    --- The latitude of the world, in degrees north of the equator. It tilts the path of the sun across
    --- the sky.
    GeographicLatitude: number
    --- The uniform light on surfaces in enclosed spaces, such as a cave, a tunnel or the underside of
    --- an overhang, which see none of the sky. A surface that sees part of the sky is lit by a blend of
    --- this and `OutdoorAmbient`. It stands in for bounced light, so a cave is dim instead of black;
    --- lower it for a darker cave. It is not clamped and can exceed 1.
    Ambient: Color3
    --- The uniform light on surfaces that see the open sky. It is equal to `Ambient` by default, so a
    --- world that sets neither is lit the same indoors and outdoors. For dark caves, lower `Ambient`
    --- alone. A part is outdoors unless terrain covers it.
    OutdoorAmbient: Color3
    --- The strength of the sun, independent of its color.
    Brightness: number
    --- The color that distant objects fade toward between `FogStart` and `FogEnd`. It is kept but not
    --- used while an `Atmosphere` is directly under `Lighting`.
    FogColor: Color3
    --- The distance at which fog begins, in meters. It is kept but not used while an `Atmosphere` is
    --- directly under `Lighting`.
    FogStart: number
    --- The distance at which fog hides everything, in meters. A value equal to or below `FogStart`
    --- turns fog off.
    FogEnd: number
    --- An adjustment to the exposure, in EV stops, added to the automatic exposure. At 0 the automatic
    --- exposure is used as measured; +1 doubles the light and -1 halves it. It is not clamped.
    ExposureCompensation: number
    --- The most the automatic exposure can darken the picture, in EV stops, as the world gets brighter.
    --- The default is about -2.75. Raise it toward 0 to keep bright places bright, so that snow glares
    --- and a desert at noon is hard to look at.
    ExposureMin: number
    --- The most the automatic exposure can brighten the picture, in EV stops, as the world gets darker.
    --- The default is about 1.5, so a night is darker than a day but still visible. Lower it for a
    --- darker night. At 0 the exposure never brightens, and a world lit a third as brightly looks a
    --- third as bright. Set `ExposureMin` and `ExposureMax` to the same value to fix the exposure
    --- there.
    ExposureMax: number
    --- How much the sky lights matte surfaces, from 0 to 1. At 0 a surface is lit only by the sun, by
    --- lights and by `Ambient`. The sky itself is drawn the same at any value.
    EnvironmentDiffuseScale: number
    --- How much shiny surfaces reflect the sky, from 0 to 1. At 0 metal reflects only the sun's
    --- highlight, which suits an interior that the sky does not reach.
    EnvironmentSpecularScale: number
    --- How soft the edges of the sun's shadows are, from 0 (as sharp as the shadow map allows) to 1 (a
    --- penumbra of a quarter of a meter on each side of the edge).
    ShadowSoftness: number
    --- Whether the sun, or the moon at night, casts shadows. Turn it off for the look of an overcast
    --- day or for a stylized world without shadows. Lights that cast shadows still do.
    GlobalShadows: boolean
    --- Whether the brightness of the picture adapts to what the camera sees, as an eye does when it
    --- moves from a cave into daylight. When it is off, the exposure is fixed, so a darker world looks
    --- darker. `ExposureCompensation` applies in both cases. A machine whose graphics settings turn
    --- automatic exposure off ignores this property.
    AutoExposure: boolean
    --- In a headset showing the player's room (`XRService.Passthrough`), how much the room shows
    --- through what the game draws, from 0 to 1. At 0 the game's world is solid and the room is seen
    --- only where nothing is drawn; at 1 only the room is seen. Tween it for a portal or a fade between
    --- the room and the game.
    XRPassthroughOpacity: number
    --- The unit vector that points from the world toward the sun. It is computed only from `ClockTime`
    --- and `GeographicLatitude`, so the same values always give the same direction.
    read SunDirection: vector
end

